::U3L1-01::Which nutrient category is an important energy source? {=Carbohydrate ~Protein ~Vitamin ~Water}

::U3L1-02::Which nutrient supports growth, maintenance, and tissue repair? {=Protein ~Water ~Mineral ~Fiber}

::U3L1-03::Which statement best describes dietary fat? {=It supports energy, cells, and absorption of certain vitamins. ~It should always be avoided. ~It is a micronutrient. ~It automatically causes poor health.}

::U3L1-04::Which two groups are micronutrients? {=Vitamins and minerals ~Carbohydrates and proteins ~Fats and water ~Fiber and calories}

::U3L1-05::Why is water essential even though it provides no calories? {=It supports fluid balance, transport, and temperature regulation. ~It replaces every nutrient. ~It builds muscle without training. ~It makes hydration needs identical.}

::U3L1-06::Fiber is best described as {=a carbohydrate component in plant foods that supports digestive and other health functions. ~a vitamin that supplies calories. ~a mineral found only in sports drinks. ~a protein supplement.}

::U3L1-07::A calorie is {=a unit used to measure energy. ~a nutrient that should be avoided. ~a type of vitamin. ~a measure of body size.}

::U3L2-01::A food Calorie generally means a {=kilocalorie ~gram ~vitamin ~serving}

::U3L2-02::Energy intake is {=energy obtained from foods and beverages ~only exercise energy ~a weight target ~activity intensity}

::U3L2-03::Energy expenditure includes {=basic functions, activity, and other physiological processes ~activity only ~food only ~body weight only}

::U3L2-04::Basal energy needs support {=essential body functions at rest ~vigorous activity only ~protein digestion only ~body-size measurement}

::U3L2-05::Metabolism refers to {=chemical processes that sustain life and use energy ~exercise duration ~one universal energy value ~body appearance}

::U3L2-06::Energy balance describes {=the relationship between intake and expenditure over time ~a command to minimize intake ~activity only ~appearance}

::U3L2-07::An estimated value is {=an informed approximation ~always exact ~a diagnosis ~a prescription}

::U3L2-08::Which variable may influence an activity estimate? {=Intensity ~Page color ~Question number ~Course title}

::U3L3-01::All nutrient values on a Nutrition Facts label relate first to the {=serving size ~package color ~advertisement ~consumer's body size}

::U3L3-02::A serving size is {=standardized label information, not an automatic personal prescription ~the amount everyone must eat ~a health rating ~an advertisement}

::U3L3-03::If one serving has 250 Calories, two servings have {=500 ~250 ~750 ~125}

::U3L3-04::If one serving has 400 mg sodium, two servings have {=800 mg ~400 mg ~600 mg ~200 mg}

::U3L3-05::If one serving has 4 g fiber, 1.5 servings have {=6 g ~4.5 g ~8 g ~2 g}

::U3L3-06::The %DV shows {=how much a nutrient in one serving contributes to a reference Daily Value ~an exact personal prescription ~total product quality ~body size}

::U3L3-07::Under the general guide, 5% DV or less is {=low ~high ~a prescription ~unsafe}

::U3L3-08::Under the general guide, 20% DV or more is {=high ~low ~zero ~personalized}

::U3L3-09::A value of 12% DV is {=between the general low and high cutoffs ~low ~high ~a diagnosis}

::U3L4Q01::Which statement best defines media literacy? {=The ability to access, analyze, evaluate, and create media messages ~Believing popular messages ~Avoiding all media ~Counting followers}

::U3L4Q02::What is a target audience? {=The group a message is designed to influence ~Everyone who owns a phone ~Only experts ~The product manufacturer}

::U3L4Q03::A personal success story is primarily what type of support? {=A testimonial ~Controlled research ~A systematic review ~A nutrition credential}

::U3L4Q04::Why can a filtered image not prove a health claim? {=Appearance does not establish health status or causation ~Filters always make claims false ~Images are never useful ~Only text can be evidence}

::U3L4Q05::What does a paid sponsorship create? {=A possible conflict of interest requiring closer evidence review ~Automatic proof of fraud ~A scientific qualification ~A control group}

::U3L4Q06::Which is the strongest evidence? {=Transparent research with appropriate methods and limitations ~A celebrity testimonial ~A large follower count ~An edited before-and-after image}

::U3L4Q07::A red-flag word such as â€œmiracleâ€ should lead a reader to do what? {=Investigate the evidence carefully ~Assume the opposite is true ~Share immediately ~Ignore the source}

::U3L4Q08::Which is a universal claim? {=This works for everyone ~Some people may respond differently ~Evidence is limited ~Consult a qualified professional}

::U3L5A01::Which goal is appropriate for this course? {=For three weeks, compare fiber on two similar food labels twice each week ~Lose ten pounds quickly ~Eat as few calories as possible ~Change body shape before an event}

::U3L5A02::A student forgets a water bottle. Which strategy best matches the barrier? {=Place the bottle beside the backpack the night before ~Track body weight daily ~Buy an expensive supplement ~Try harder without changing a cue}

::U3L5A03::A goal is to bring a water bottle four school days each week. Which monitoring method best matches it? {=A weekday behavior checklist ~Daily body weight ~A calorie-deficit chart ~An appearance photograph}

::U3L5A04::Which baseline is non-sensitive and useful? {=I currently check food labels only occasionally ~My exact body weight is... ~My private diagnosis is... ~My full daily food diary is...}

::U3L5A05::Which source should most strongly guide a nutrition plan? {=A qualified evidence-based source that states methods and limits ~A paid influencer testimonial ~A filtered transformation photo ~The post with the most followers}

::U3L5A06::Which feature most improves plan sustainability? {=Strategies fit time, cost, access, culture, preference, and schedule ~The plan requires perfection every day ~The plan eliminates a nutrient group ~The plan depends on one costly product}

::U3L5A07::When a planned behavior is repeatedly missed, what is the best response? {=Evaluate barriers and adjust the strategy ~Declare the whole plan a failure ~Add weight tracking ~Adopt an influencer's universal plan}

::U3L5A08::Which correction best repairs a flawed plan to skip breakfast and weigh daily? {=Choose a safe behavior goal and monitor completion of that behavior ~Keep skipping breakfast but weigh weekly ~Replace breakfast with a supplement ~Use a lower calorie target}

